1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,874
By Friday afternoon Sam had eleven hook lines drafted for Harlan, each one a little

2
00:00:04,874 --> 00:00:06,292
more clever than the last.

3
00:00:06,292 --> 00:00:10,652
Reading them back, he felt the problem before he could name it: every line could

4
00:00:10,652 --> 00:00:14,087
have been written for almost any product that promised comfort.

5
00:00:14,087 --> 00:00:18,556
Nothing in any of them named the actual moment a real Harlan customer was standing

6
00:00:18,556 --> 00:00:18,720
in.

7
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:20,028
Line twelve was plainer.

8
00:00:20,028 --> 00:00:24,280
"Your feet don't have to feel like this by hour eight." No wordplay, no twist.

9
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:28,859
It named a specific hour on a specific kind of shift, and it was the only one of the

10
00:00:28,859 --> 00:00:30,767
twelve that survived a second read.

11
00:00:30,767 --> 00:00:35,291
Every ad lives or dies in about half a second, and that half second has a name: the

12
00:00:35,291 --> 00:00:39,707
hook, the opening line or image that has to stop a scroll before anything else in

13
00:00:39,707 --> 00:00:40,851
the ad gets a chance.

14
00:00:40,851 --> 00:00:43,359
Most people think a hook is a writing problem.

15
00:00:43,359 --> 00:00:45,321
Write it sharper, punchier, funnier.

16
00:00:45,321 --> 00:00:49,137
It's actually a targeting problem wearing a writing problem's clothes.

17
00:00:49,137 --> 00:00:53,552
A hook's job is to interrupt one specific thought someone was already having, the

18
00:00:53,552 --> 00:00:57,095
exact thought Sam's eleven drafts missed and his twelfth one hit.

19
00:00:57,095 --> 00:01:00,693
Get the thought wrong, and no amount of cleverness saves the line.

20
00:01:00,693 --> 00:01:05,108
Get it right, and a plain sentence outperforms a brilliant one aimed at the wrong

21
00:01:05,108 --> 00:01:05,490
person.

22
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:10,366
If you only take one pattern from this table into this week's batch, take pattern

23
00:01:10,366 --> 00:01:10,900
interrupt.

24
00:01:10,900 --> 00:01:15,227
It's the only one of the six that works regardless of which rung the viewer's on,

25
00:01:15,227 --> 00:01:17,256
because it isn't arguing anything yet.

26
00:01:17,256 --> 00:01:21,529
It's just earning the half second everything else here needs in order to land at

27
00:01:21,529 --> 00:01:21,743
all.

28
00:01:21,743 --> 00:01:26,016
Start there if you're not sure which rung most of your traffic actually sits on.

29
00:01:26,016 --> 00:01:30,396
If you want the full six-pattern breakdown: problem-agitate names a feeling before

30
00:01:30,396 --> 00:01:34,455
it's been put into words, direct address says "you, specifically" instead of

31
00:01:34,455 --> 00:01:38,781
speaking to everyone, spec proof answers "does this actually work" with a number,

32
00:01:38,781 --> 00:01:43,054
myth-bust takes something the audience already believes and turns it around, and

33
00:01:43,054 --> 00:01:47,167
objection answer meets the one hesitation stopping a warm viewer from buying.

34
00:01:47,167 --> 00:01:51,653
But the decision that actually matters this week is simpler than memorising all six:

35
00:01:51,653 --> 00:01:56,086
pick the one pattern that matches the rung you're weakest on, not whichever one you

36
00:01:56,086 --> 00:01:57,368
personally like writing.

37
00:01:57,368 --> 00:02:01,428
One more flag: the five non-interrupt patterns don't transfer cleanly across

38
00:02:01,428 --> 00:02:01,855
formats.

39
00:02:01,855 --> 00:02:06,021
Spec proof reads naturally as text over a static image; spoken flat by a video

40
00:02:06,021 --> 00:02:08,852
avatar, the same line can sound like a warning label.

41
00:02:08,852 --> 00:02:10,881
Sections three and four cover the fix.

42
00:02:11,431 --> 00:02:16,022
This is the actual artifact, and notice the shape of it: one pattern per rung, not

43
00:02:16,022 --> 00:02:18,149
one pattern stretched across all five.

44
00:02:18,149 --> 00:02:22,628
Each line traces straight back to the product truth document and the ladder from

45
00:02:22,628 --> 00:02:23,356
last lecture.

46
00:02:23,356 --> 00:02:26,603
The spec proof line uses the exact number Dana can defend.

47
00:02:26,603 --> 00:02:31,081
The objection-answer line assumes a real hesitation, not a generic nudge to buy.

48
00:02:31,081 --> 00:02:33,321
Nothing here is clever for its own sake.

49
00:02:33,321 --> 00:02:36,792
Every line is doing one specific job, for one specific person.

50
00:02:36,792 --> 00:02:40,263
Notice, too, that solution-aware gets two rows instead of one.

51
00:02:40,263 --> 00:02:44,797
That's deliberate: it's the widest rung, so it earns two genuinely different bets

52
00:02:44,797 --> 00:02:49,388
rather than one guess stretched to cover a crowd that doesn't think as one person.

53
00:02:49,388 --> 00:02:53,811
And notice what's missing: most-aware, the top rung from last lecture's ladder.

54
00:02:53,811 --> 00:02:54,762
Not an oversight.

55
00:02:54,762 --> 00:02:59,409
Most-aware audiences are existing customers, reached through retargeting and email,

56
00:02:59,409 --> 00:03:03,552
not cold hook-driven creative, so this matrix doesn't need a row for them.

57
00:03:03,552 --> 00:03:04,951
A retention matrix would.

58
00:03:04,951 --> 00:03:09,374
If you're only testing one thing this week, don't try to write all five rows at

59
00:03:09,374 --> 00:03:09,654
once.

60
00:03:09,654 --> 00:03:14,133
Start with whichever row matches the rung carrying most of your current traffic,

61
00:03:14,133 --> 00:03:18,555
write that one line properly, and let the rest of the matrix fill in as you go.

62
00:03:19,105 --> 00:03:23,680
Here's the tell that separates a real hook from something that only sounds like one.

63
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:28,091
"You deserve comfort" is true of almost every product ever sold, which means it's

64
00:03:28,091 --> 00:03:29,724
aimed at no one in particular.

65
00:03:29,724 --> 00:03:34,026
"By hour eight" names an actual moment a real person on a real shift recognises

66
00:03:34,026 --> 00:03:34,571
instantly.

67
00:03:34,571 --> 00:03:38,764
Specificity is the whole trick, and it's also the thing a generic prompt to a

68
00:03:38,764 --> 00:03:42,249
generator will quietly strip out unless you ask for it directly.

69
00:03:42,249 --> 00:03:46,769
Here's a fast test you can run on any hook before it ships: could this line run for

70
00:03:46,769 --> 00:03:49,655
a completely different product with a five-word edit?

71
00:03:49,655 --> 00:03:53,032
If yes, it's generic, no matter how good it sounds read aloud.

72
00:03:53,032 --> 00:03:55,755
"Hour eight" barely survives that test for Harlan.

73
00:03:55,755 --> 00:03:58,314
It doesn't survive it at all for anything else.

74
00:03:58,314 --> 00:04:00,928
Run that same test on the eleven drafts Sam cut.

75
00:04:00,928 --> 00:04:05,393
Every one of them survives a swap to a completely unrelated product with barely an

76
00:04:05,393 --> 00:04:09,968
edit, which is exactly how he knew, before any data existed, that they weren't hooks

77
00:04:09,968 --> 00:04:10,185
yet.

78
00:04:10,185 --> 00:04:11,819
They were just nice sentences.

79
00:04:11,819 --> 00:04:15,087
Try it on your own last batch before you generate a new one.

80
00:04:15,087 --> 00:04:19,225
Read each opening line and ask whether it would survive being swapped onto a

81
00:04:19,225 --> 00:04:21,567
competitor's product with one word changed.

82
00:04:21,567 --> 00:04:25,760
Anything that survives is worth rewriting before it ships again, not reusing.

83
00:04:26,310 --> 00:04:29,295
One honest limit before you build angles on top of this.

84
00:04:29,295 --> 00:04:33,719
Everything on this matrix is an informed guess about which pattern fits which rung,

85
00:04:33,719 --> 00:04:36,544
built from the ladder and the product truth document.

86
00:04:36,544 --> 00:04:40,915
It isn't verified yet, and it shouldn't be treated as settled until a real test in

87
00:04:40,915 --> 00:04:42,087
Section seven says so.

88
00:04:42,087 --> 00:04:44,486
The matrix earns you a better starting batch.

89
00:04:44,486 --> 00:04:45,978
It doesn't replace the test.

90
00:04:45,978 --> 00:04:50,349
It's worth remembering exactly why that matters, because it's the same lesson from

91
00:04:50,349 --> 00:04:54,560
Section one, showing up again in a new place: a well-reasoned guess about which

92
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:56,372
pattern will win is still a guess.

93
00:04:56,372 --> 00:05:00,370
It's a much better guess than a blank page, and it is still not the answer.

94
00:05:00,370 --> 00:05:03,195
Sam's twelfth line felt right the moment he wrote it.

95
00:05:03,195 --> 00:05:07,565
It still has to earn that feeling in a real test before anyone trusts it with real

96
00:05:07,565 --> 00:05:11,456
spend, which is a different claim entirely than "it read well on a Friday

97
00:05:11,456 --> 00:05:12,043
afternoon."

98
00:05:12,593 --> 00:05:17,042
Next lecture is where all of this finally becomes a number: one product truth

99
00:05:17,042 --> 00:05:21,607
document, five awareness levels, six hook patterns, turned into twenty testable

100
00:05:21,607 --> 00:05:24,149
angles you can actually hand to a generator.

101
00:05:24,149 --> 00:05:28,888
Nothing about that step requires you to be a better writer than you are right now.

102
00:05:28,888 --> 00:05:33,395
It mostly requires you to keep asking which rung, honestly, instead of writing

103
00:05:33,395 --> 00:05:35,360
whatever line comes to mind first.

104
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:36,862
Bring the matrix with you.

105
00:05:36,862 --> 00:05:40,329
It's the multiplication table the angle map is about to run.

106
00:05:40,329 --> 00:05:45,183
Before then, pick the one rung your product gets the most cold traffic on, and write

107
00:05:45,183 --> 00:05:47,899
one hook for it using the pattern that matches.

108
00:05:47,899 --> 00:05:52,348
That single line is the first real row of your own hook matrix, and it's what

109
00:05:52,348 --> 00:05:54,891
section 2.6 turns into part of artifact B01.
